      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to identify the number of distinct equilateral triangles that can be formed with at least two vertices from the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\). We will consider different cases based on the number of vertices in the equilateral triangle.\n\n### Case 1: Three vertices of the equilateral triangle are the same\nIf an equilateral triangle has three vertices that are the same, it is simply a set of three consecutive vertices of the polygon. There are 9 such equilateral triangles:\n\\[\nA_1A_2A_3, A_2A_3A_4, A_3A_4A_5, A_4A_5A_6, A_5A_6A_7, A_6A_7A_8, A_7A_8A_9, A_8A_9A_1, A_9A_1A_2\n\\]\nEach of these triangles is distinct because they have the same vertices.\n\n### Case 2: Two vertices of the equilateral triangle are the same\nIf an equilateral triangle has two vertices that are the same, the third vertex must be opposite to the first two. For example, if \\(A_1A_2A_3\\) is the equilateral triangle, then \\(A_3A_6A_9\\) is the third equilateral triangle. To count these, we note that for each vertex \\(A_i\\), there are exactly 3 such triangles (one for each position of the common vertex). This gives us:\n\\[\n9 \\times 3 = 27 \\text{ equilateral triangles}\n\\]\nHowever, we have already counted the triangles in Case 1, so we need to subtract the 9 common triangles:\n\\[\n27 - 9 = 18 \\text{ distinct equilateral triangles}\n\\]\n\n### Case 3: None of the vertices of the equilateral triangle are the same\nIn this case, the vertices of the equilateral triangle must be chosen such that no two vertices are consecutive. We can use the principle of counting by choosing 3 vertices from 9 such that no two are consecutive. This is equivalent to choosing 3 vertices from a set of 9 - 2(k-1) vertices, where k is the number of vertices between consecutive chosen vertices. We can simplify this to choosing 3 vertices from 6 different positions:\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} = 20\n\\]\nHowever, this counts the triangles where the third vertex is also one of the three chosen vertices. We need to subtract these duplicates. The number of duplicates is the number of ways to choose 2 vertices from 3 and the third from the remaining 6:\n\\[\n3 \\times \\binom{6}{2} = 3 \\times 15 = 45\n\\]\nBut we have already counted the common triangles in Case 1, so we need to subtract 9:\n\\[\n20 - 9 = 11\n\\]\nBut we need to add back the 9 common triangles we subtracted twice:\n\\[\n11 + 9 = 20\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\nCombining all the cases, the total number of distinct equilateral triangles is:\n\\[\n9 + 18 + 11 = 38\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{38}\n\\]",
