      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine how many distinct equilateral triangles can be formed in the plane of a regular nonagon (9-sided polygon) with at least two vertices in the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\).\n\nFirst, let's consider the vertices of the nonagon: \\(A_1, A_2, A_3, \\ldots, A_9\\). The goal is to find all sets of three vertices \\(\\{A_i, A_j, A_k\\}\\) such that the triangle formed by these vertices is equilateral.\n\nThe side length of a regular nonagon is the same, and the distance between any two vertices \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_j\\) is the same. Let's denote this distance by \\(s\\). An equilateral triangle in the plane will have all sides of length \\(s\\).\n\nTo form an equilateral triangle, we need to select three vertices such that the distance between each pair of vertices is \\(s\\). The only possible equilateral triangles in a regular nonagon are those where three consecutive vertices form an equilateral triangle. This is because the distance between any two consecutive vertices of a regular nonagon is the same and equal to the side length \\(s\\).\n\nThere are 9 such equilateral triangles, namely:\n1. \\(\\{A_1, A_3, A_5\\}\\)\n2. \\(\\{A_2, A_4, A_6\\}\\)\n3. \\(\\{A_3, A_5, A_7\\}\\)\n4. \\(\\{A_4, A_6, A_8\\}\\)\n5. \\(\\{A_5, A_7, A_9\\}\\)\n6. \\(\\{A_6, A_8, A_1\\}\\)\n7. \\(\\{A_7, A_9, A_2\\}\\)\n8. \\(\\{A_8, A_1, A_3\\}\\)\n9. \\(\\{A_9, A_2, A_4\\}\\)\n\nEach of these sets of three consecutive vertices forms a distinct equilateral triangle. Since there are 9 such sets, the number of distinct equilateral triangles in the plane of the polygon is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
